+# GSQ Radeon Pro 580 + CPU
+
+This module targets the Intel iMac's discrete Radeon Pro 580 (8 GiB) and the
+existing GSQ AVX2 worker pool. It is an opt-in candidate until live cluster
+measurements are recorded. It requires the accompanying integration patch,
+not just copying the module into an unmodified installer.
+
+The first three assigned units are uploaded into separate private Metal
+buffers. Remaining units are borrowed by the existing CPU backend. A unit is
+never computed by both. `submit -> CPU run -> collect -> sum` overlaps the
+independent partials without adding threads. Existing Q8_K wire input remains
+validated and directly usable. The original IQ2/IQ3/IQ4_NL/Q2_0 weights and
+frozen numerical tolerances are unchanged.
+
+## Explicit preparation and memory ownership
+
+`fr_backend_prepare(..., &host_retained)` extends setup without changing the
+ordinary `load` borrowing contract. Successful GPU preparation sets
+`host_retained=0`; the caller unmaps the CPU bytes immediately. `unload` releases
+the owner selected during preparation. The same helper is used by real loading,
+fixtures, synthetic demo and calibration. Calibration checks CPU pages with
+mincore and accounts for the allocated private GPU buffers separately from RSS.
+This does not claim that the OS can never evict a GPU resource. Capacity remains
+conservative: no extra system RAM capacity is invented from available VRAM.
+
+Three units use 6,554,910,720 bytes of private device storage. Each buffer is
+below the device's maximum single-buffer length. A 512 MiB budget reserve is
+required. Transfers use a bounded 64 MiB staging buffer during preparation.
+Unmapped file pages can remain in the OS's reclaimable file cache; there is no
+retained CPU mapping or heap copy of GPU weights.
+
+The device, queue, pipelines and work buffers persist. Model weights persist
+across compatible sessions; ordinary requests only transfer activations and
+routing. A changed assignment, explicit benchmark, or process restart naturally
+requires preparation of its new weights. INFO backend exposes exclusive CPU/GPU
+masks, device weight bytes, and cumulative upload bytes. The latter must remain
+unchanged across chats using the same assignment.
+
+## Hardware and arithmetic
+
+64-lane SIMD groups were verified on the real GPU. Gate/up dequantization reuses
+pinned ggml IQ2/IQ3 functions and tables (MIT, GGML-LICENSE), amortized over up to
+four/eight routed tokens. Down projections retain GSQ's original layout. Hidden
+quantization propagates nonfinite values to the output check, including zero
+gates. Errors leave caller output untouched and submitted GPU work is drained.
+Unsupported devices are rejected before use. Shared expert gates must be zero;
+this matches GSQ, whose shared expert remains on the head.
+
+Primary references:
+- https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/3DDrawing/Conceptual/MTLBestPracticesGuide/PersistentObjects.html
+- https://developer.apple.com/documentation/metal/setting-resource-storage-modes
+- https://developer.apple.com/metal/Metal-Shading-Language-Specification.pdf
+
+## Reproduction and evidence
+
+Workspace helpers in build/gsq-radeon:
+- integrate.py: prepare isolated GSQ tree and embedded shader source.
+- remote.py build/check/validate/tests: build, scalar SELFTEST, real-weight
+  3-GPU/5-CPU comparison, host-mapping/upload checks, head/node tests.
+- deploy.py gpu/cpu/retain: reversible iMac deployment, CPU rollback, source retention.
+- cluster.py measure/stop/start/finish: controlled live generation and warmup lifecycle.
+
+SELFTEST covers all 48 layers, widths 1..8, a nonadjacent ownership ID, duplicate
+routes, nonfinite input and corrupt scales, zero gates, unsupported shared gates,
+and unchanged output on failure. Real validation compares all 48 layers and
+widths 1..8 over eight actual units, checks three released host mappings, constant
+upload bytes across requests and complete unload. The CPU/scalar reference is
+not replaced. Initial ABBA component measurements are in hybrid.log; live tok/s
+must be assessed separately from those microbenchmarks.
